---
// Obligations Inbox — RETIRED 2026-09-15, superseded by /prototypes/obligation-tracking.
//
// Kept, not deleted, so the two stabs can be compared. What survived: the threading — an
// event is the parent and the duties it raised are its children. What did not: the mail
// metaphor around it. Open/Seen, the per-notice filing verbs and the derived severity
// ordering are all gone in the replacement. The notes below describe this page as built.
//
// Obligations Inbox — the awareness surface for the Obligation record type.
//
// WHY AN INBOX AND NOT A DASHBOARD. Five earlier dashboard variants all failed the same way:
// they arranged 402 obligations, and arranging a registry is not the same as telling somebody
// what happened. The team's own framing (2026-09-06) was the fix — make it work like the
// Evidence Inbox: a queue of things that arrived, threaded, with the reason each one is in
// front of you.
//
// THE THREADING IS THE IDEA. A TRIGGER is the parent and the obligations it raises are its
// children. Someone sees a burrowing owl; that one event raises thirty duties, five of them
// owed on a clock. Listing those thirty as separate rows is how an inbox turns back into a
// registry, so the event is the row and the duties are the thread. "Why am I seeing this"
// stops being a sentence someone wrote and becomes the structure of the page.
//
// PRIORITY IS DERIVED FROM THE REGISTRY, from the two signals the team named: a strict
// deadline (26 of the 50 Notify rows are owed in a day or less) and take or injury (20 rows).
// A trigger inherits the strongest signal among the obligations it raised — and it DECAYS
// with the age of the event, because a 24-hour notice on an owl seen 40 minutes ago is open
// while the same duty on a season that started last week is background. Without the decay all
// eight triggers ranked "now" and the signal said nothing.
//
// WHAT IS REAL: every obligation a trigger raises is matched out of the 402-row registry by a
// real predicate, and the conditions, classes, notice windows, commitment codes and
// commitment titles are all registry data — including the drivers each row links to, which is
// what answers "what is making me do this".
//
// WHAT IS EXAMPLE, and said so on the page: which triggers fired, when, where and who
// reported them. The registry carries no in-effect conditions and no observations, so the
// events themselves are authored. Nothing uses Date.now() — see NOW in the data module.
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import EsaBadge from '@esa/ecology/esa-badge.astro';
import EsaAlertBox from '@esa/ecology/esa-alert-box.astro';
import BcnInboxWorkspace from '../../components/bcn/BcnInboxWorkspace.astro';
import { TOTALS } from '../../data/obligation-triggers';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/base';

const projectNav = [
  {
    id: 'tracking',
    title: 'Tracking',
    icon: 'radar',
    expanded: true,
    active: true,
    items: [
      { id: 'tr-actions', label: 'Actions' },
      { id: 'tr-obligations', label: 'Obligations', active: true },
      { id: 'tr-evidence', label: 'Evidence Inbox' },
    ],
  },
];

const obligationBase = withBase('/prototypes/data-catalog-obligation');
---

<!-- manifest:
  layout: AppShell > PageLayout > stack(md)          # SPINE
  sections:
    - inbox workspace -> bcn-inbox-workspace         # fixed two-pane frame; every thread server-rendered
        - queue       -> bcn-inbox-queue             #   left: triggers in relative date buckets, urgency-sorted
        - thread      -> bcn-inbox-thread            #   right: one trigger, the obligations it raised, their drivers
  title-badge: esa-badge                             # notices owed right now
-->

<AppShell
  title="Obligations Inbox — Beacon"
  tenantName="DWR"
  projectName="Delta Conveyance"
  navSections={projectNav}
>
  <PageLayout
    title="Obligations Inbox"
    icon="inbox"
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Tracking', href: '#tracking' }, { label: 'Obligations Inbox' }]}
  >
    {/* The count rides the page title so the area is named once. It counts what is OWED,
        not what is queued — the number a person acts on. */}
    <Fragment slot="title-badge">
      {/* data-inbox-total is the controller's hook: it re-labels this as notices are filed,
          so the headline number and the queue can never look like they disagree. */}
      <span data-inbox-total>
        <EsaBadge
          value={`${TOTALS.needNow} notices owed`}
          variant={TOTALS.needNow ? 'danger' : 'secondary'}
          size="sm"
        />
      </span>
    </Fragment>

    {/* RETIRED, not deleted. Kept so the two stabs can be read side by side — what the
        threading got right, and what the mail metaphor got wrong. */}
    <EsaAlertBox variant="warning" heading="Superseded by Obligation Tracking">
      This was the first stab at the awareness surface. The threading survives; the mail metaphor
      does not — nothing is owed to a feed, and a standing duty is still in force tomorrow whether
      or not you marked it seen. The Open/Seen pivot, the filing verbs and the severity ordering are
      all gone in <a href={withBase('/prototypes/obligation-tracking')}>Obligation Tracking</a>,
      which replaces them with four views and carries the registry as a read-only section.
    </EsaAlertBox>

    <BcnInboxWorkspace obligationBase={obligationBase} />
  </PageLayout>
</AppShell>
